FILE: examples/resizer/index.html
FEATURE: resizer-example
DATE: 2026-08-27
PRIOR STATE: did not exist

OLD_SECTION
(no file — Resizer was the only repaired module with no page under examples/)

NEW_SECTION
A five-section page built on examples/shared/example.css: a hero whose headline box is
itself the component, a playground with a live control for every config property, a
split view driven by a consumer-supplied grip, a four-cell grid covering every edge, a
teardown panel, and a validation grid of thirteen deliberately-bad calls.

REASON
CLAUDE.md requires a runnable example of every module, and Resizer had none. It was
also the reason the resizer-fixes work could only be verified statically: with no page
to load the module there was no way to confirm a real drag, or that resizer.css
resolves at runtime under a live data-library-root.

Section choices follow what the repair actually changed, so the page doubles as its
demonstration:

  - The hero uses direction:"both" with all four thresholds set, because the pre-fix
    module could not do either — the drag arithmetic ignored the drag, and the module
    threw before it set any state.
  - Section 03 exists solely for the left/top edge inversion, which is new behaviour.
    Before the fix those two position settings produced controls that shrank the
    element when dragged outward.
  - Section 02 exercises config.myResizeHandler, which was previously validated,
    stored, and used for nothing. The grip is deliberately a sibling of the resized
    panel rather than a child, and deliberately contains an icon, because those are
    the two things the old $$.attachEventHandler binding could not handle.
  - Section 04 exists because destroy() is new, and because initialize() used to
    append a duplicate handle, listener set and stylesheet when called twice.
  - Section 05 includes `resizeHandlerProperties = {}` and an unknown-key case
    specifically: the first threw a bare TypeError before the fix, the second was
    silently ignored.

Three page-level CSS decisions are load-bearing and are commented in the file and
explained in the README, because a consumer hits all three:

  1. A 14px hit area with a 3px visible line inside it. The library's 2px handle is
     structurally correct but hard to grab, and has no resting appearance at all.
  2. Handle rules scoped to a page class (.rz) rather than written as bare
     .resizeHandle. resizer.css is injected during initialize(), so it lands after
     the page's own <style> block and would win every specificity tie.
  3. Each box in section 03 anchored to the far side of its cell with flexbox. The
     module writes a size and never a position, so without anchoring a left- or
     top-handled box still resizes but visibly grows the wrong way — which would read
     as the inversion fix not working.
